(52, 'How many borrower tickets are typically issued to registered students in the Federal University Lokoja Library?', 'One', 'Two', 'Three', 'None', 'B'),
(53, 'What is the purpose of the Reserve Unit in a library?', 'To borrow books', 'To consult or use certain materials within the library', 'To sell rare books', 'To repair damaged books', 'B'),
(54, 'How do patrons access books from the Reserve Unit?', 'By filling out a form and exchanging it for the book', 'By purchasing the book', 'By borrowing the book for an extended period', 'By downloading the book online', 'A'),
(55, 'What is the main difference between materials in the circulation section and the reserve section of a library?', 'Circulation materials are free to borrow, while reserve materials are not', 'Circulation materials are always in print, while reserve materials are out of print', 'Circulation materials are rare books, while reserve materials are common', 'Circulation materials are for sale, while reserve materials are free', 'A'),
(56, 'Which library service involves taking patrons around the library to educate them on how to use resources and facilities effectively?', 'Current awareness services', 'Selective Dissemination of Information (SDI) Services', 'Loaning of laptops', 'Library orientation', 'D'),
(57, 'What kind of services are aimed at keeping library users informed about new resources and developments in their areas of interest?', 'Loaning of laptops', 'E-learning literacy services', 'Current awareness services', 'Library orientation', 'C'),
(58, 'Which library service helps patrons stay up-to-date with the latest technological skills and knowledge?', 'Library orientation', 'E-learning literacy services', 'Loaning of laptops', 'Selective Dissemination of Information (SDI) Services', 'B'),
(59, 'What service involves providing information to library users based on their specific interests and needs?', 'Loaning of laptops', 'E-learning literacy services', 'Current awareness services', 'Selective Dissemination of Information (SDI) Services', 'D'),
(60, 'Which library service includes the temporary provision of laptops to patrons for use within the library?', 'Selective Dissemination of Information (SDI) Services', 'Loaning of laptops', 'E-learning literacy services', 'Current awareness services', 'B'),
(61, 'What is the main purpose of historical studies?', 'To predict the future', 'To uncover hidden mysteries of the past', 'To improve the present and future based on past lessons', 'To create fictional stories based on the past', 'C'),
(62, 'What is the origin of the word \"History\"?', 'Greek word \"Historia\"', 'Arabic word \"Ta\'arikh\"', 'Latin word \"Historicus\"', 'Sanskrit word \"Itihasa\"', 'A'),
(63, 'How does E. H. Carr define history?', 'As a process of inquiry into the past of man in society', 'As a set of facts about the past', 'As a fictional narrative about the past', 'As a method for predicting the future', 'A'),
(64, 'What criticism is often directed at E. H. Carr\'s definition of history?', 'Overemphasis on the role of oral tradition', 'Lack of emphasis on the process of inquiry', 'Overemphasis on the result of the inquiry', 'Lack of clarity and coherence in the definition', 'C'),
(65, 'According to Abubakar (2005), what does history deal with?', 'Artistic expressions within a society', 'The documentation, exposition, and analysis of events and development within a society', 'Fictional stories based on historical events', 'The interpretation of ancient artifacts', 'B'),
(66, 'How does Memeje (1988) define history?', 'As a body of knowledge about the future', 'The documentation of past acts of man in society', 'As a collection of fictional stories about the past', 'As a record of daily events in a society', 'B'),
(67, 'What distinguishes history from storytelling?', 'Inquiry and presentation of facts', 'Artistic expression and creativity', 'Mere concoction and fabrication of events', 'Detailed character development', 'A'),
(68, 'Why is knowledge of history considered important?', 'To create fictional narratives', 'To understand past events and illuminate the present', 'To predict the future based on past experiences', 'To fabricate historical events', 'B'),
(69, 'What does the scope of history encompass?', 'Only political events and leaders', 'A narrow range of topics within human endeavor', 'A wide spectrum of human activities in the past', 'Only military and warfare-related subjects', 'C'),
(70, 'Why do historians develop specialties within the discipline?', 'To focus on a single historical event', 'To exclude other historians from their work', 'To narrow the scope of historical studies', 'To study particular groups, events, or periods', 'D'),
(71, 'Is history commonly categorized as an art or science?', 'Neither, it stands alone as a separate discipline', 'Both, as it incorporates elements of both art and science', 'Exclusively as an art form', 'Exclusively as a science', 'B'),
(72, 'Who regarded history as \"science no more no less\"?', 'Commager', 'Ifemeje', 'Carr', 'Daignshill ove', 'C'),
(73, 'According to Commager, is history considered a science?', 'Yes, it is seen as a true science', 'No, it is viewed as an art form', 'Yes, but only in specific contexts', 'No, it is not a field of study', 'B'),
(74, 'According to Leopold Von Ranke, is history more of an art or science?', 'More of an art', 'More of a science', 'An equal blend of both', 'Neither, it stands alone as a separate discipline', 'A'),
(75, 'What is one reason history is considered an art?', 'It involves scientific experiments', 'It can control the material it uses', 'It deals with specific and unique events', 'It establishes general laws', 'C'),
(76, 'How does the subjectivity of historians affect the field of history?', 'It enhances objectivity', 'It introduces bias and prejudice', 'It reduces subjectivity to insignificant dimensions', 'It follows scientific laws', 'B'),
(77, 'What distinguishes natural sciences like biology from history?', 'The ability to predict future outcomes', 'The use of fragmentary evidences', 'The focus on general laws', 'The yield of useful inventions', 'C'),
(78, 'What is the main concern of historians, as opposed to scientists?', 'Predicting the future', 'Understanding the past and reconstructing it', 'Formulating scientific laws', 'Inventing useful technologies', 'B'),
(79, 'How do postmodernists view the nature of history?', 'As an objective representation of the past', 'As a field with the ability to predict the future', 'As a scientific discipline', 'As an artistic representation of the past', 'D'),
(80, 'According to the postmodernists, what does history reveal more about?', 'The objective nature of the past', 'The historian\'s personal views and perspective', 'The general laws of human behavior', 'The scientific laws governing history', 'B'),
(81, 'What are sources of historical knowledge?', 'Tangible and intangible things', 'Written documents and statistics', 'Artifacts and archaeological findings', 'Oral traditions and personal letters', 'A'),
(82, 'What is oral tradition in the context of historical sources?', 'Documents written on various materials', 'Information passed down orally', 'Artifacts used for historical research', 'Personal letters exchanged between individuals', 'B'),
(83, 'Why is oral tradition important for African history?', 'Because it is always reliable', 'Because it\'s free from exaggeration', 'Because it allows reconstruction of history', 'Because it is not prone to misinformation', 'C'),
(84, 'What are some challenges associated with oral tradition?', 'Susceptibility to exaggeration', 'Fading away and becoming illegible', 'Inability to access easily', 'Lack of cross-checking with other sources', 'A'),
(85, 'What are examples of written sources in history?', 'Artifacts and archaeological findings', 'Personal letters and diaries', 'Proclamations and laws', 'Folktales and legends', 'B'),
(86, 'Why are written sources accorded respect by historians?', 'Because they are always reliable', 'Because they are easier to use', 'Because they are immune to misrepresentation', 'Because they are stored easily', 'B'),
(87, 'What kind of information can be derived from archaeological findings?', 'Information about human societies', 'Information about political events', 'Information about written records', 'Information about oral traditions', 'A'),
(88, 'How does archaeology contribute to understanding historical epochs?', 'By studying human remains', 'By analyzing statistical data', 'By examining artifacts and material remains', 'By reading personal letters', 'C');
INSERT INTO `gst_102` (`id`, `question`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `answer`) VALUES
(1, 'What are some key components of research ?', 'Theory/Theoretical Models & Approaches', 'Analysis of Data', 'Research Proposal/Design', 'Documentation in each', 'C'),
(2, 'In the context of essential requirements for a long essay, what is \"Viva\"?', 'Content & Organization', 'General revision', 'Format details', 'A part of research ethics', 'B'),
(3, 'Where should you focus on \"Language & Style\" in a long essay?', 'In the discussion of results', 'In the research problem/focus section', 'Throughout the essay', 'In the analysis of data', 'C'),
(4, 'What is the primary purpose of discussing \"Research Ethics\"?', 'To present the findings', 'To conduct general revision', 'To analyze research data', 'To ensure ethical research practices', 'D'),
(5, 'What does the term \"Content & Organization\" in the long essay refer to?', 'The introduction and conclusion', 'The thesis statement', 'The language used in the essay', 'How well the essay is structured', 'D'),
(6, 'How does a formal essay differ from an informal essay?', 'Formal essays are code-mapped and colloquial', 'Informal essays lack emotion', 'Formal essays are non-fictional and elevated', 'Informal essays are non-fictional and use formal language', 'C'),
(7, 'What types of discourse do informal essays usually address?', 'Society-based and community concerns', 'Personal interests and limited audiences', 'Academic topics and factual content', 'High-stake public matters', 'B'),
(8, 'What characterizes a classroom essay?', 'Extended content and detailed explanation', 'Limited time, space, and resources', 'Academic dissertation and thesis', 'Short fictional stories', 'B'),
(9, 'How can a long essay be described in terms of content and length?', 'Concise and brief', 'Extensive and detailed', 'Imaginary and fictional', 'Reserved and non-expository', 'B'),
(10, 'Which of the following is an example of a long essay?', 'A news commentary', 'An academic dissertation', 'A personal open letter', 'A brief incident report', 'B'),
(11, 'Which type of essay is characterized by a storytelling style?', 'Descriptive Essay', 'Expository Essay', 'Argumentative Essay', 'Narrative Essay', 'D'),
(12, 'What is a distinctive feature of the narrative essay?', 'Emphasis on facts and figures', 'Emphasis on formal language', 'Flow of ideas in a chronological pattern', 'Focus on creating vivid images', 'D'),
(13, 'Which type of essay aims to create a specific impression of the subject in the reader\'s mind?', 'Expository Essay', 'Descriptive Essay', 'Argumentative Essay', 'Narrative Essay', 'B'),
(14, 'What is the primary focus of an expository essay?', 'Emotion and storytelling style', 'Exposing the subject', 'Presenting arguments and justification', 'Creating vivid images', 'B'),
(15, 'Which type of essay deals with controversial issues and aims to persuade readers to support a particular viewpoint?', 'Descriptive Essay', 'Argumentative Essay', 'Expository Essay', 'Narrative Essay', 'B'),
(16, 'What does the subject matter of an essay encompass?', 'Choice of linguistic style and viewpoint', 'A concise essay title', 'A broad area of knowledge', 'Relevance to the topic', 'C'),
(17, 'What is the role of a thesis statement in essay writing?', 'Creating vivid images and descriptive language', 'Establishing a formal tone', 'Presenting arguments and justification', 'Outlining the proposed points', 'C'),
(18, 'How is an outline structured for an essay?', 'Numbered sequentially with various markers', 'Containing only main points', 'Written in paragraphs and phrases', 'Fixed and stereotypical structure', 'A'),
(19, 'What is the significance of a quality outline in essay writing?', 'A bad outline can still result in a good essay', 'The outline is inflexible', 'The outline is often stereotypical', 'A quality outline leads to a good essay', 'D'),
(20, 'What can be inferred about the flexibility of an outline?', 'The outline structure is always fixed and stereotypical', 'The outline is sacrosanct', 'The quality of an outline is unimportant', 'The outline is dynamic and flexible', 'D'),
(21, 'What is the primary function of a thesis statement in an essay?', 'To provide background information', 'To explain complex terms', 'To serve as the essay\'s entry point', 'To express the central idea and focus of the essay', 'D'),
(22, 'How does the strength of a thesis statement relate to the outcome of an essay?', 'It has no direct impact on the essay\'s outcome', 'A strong thesis statement leads to a stronger essay', 'A weak thesis statement results in a stronger essay', 'A strong thesis statement leads to a weaker essay', 'B'),
(23, 'What is the main purpose of an essay introduction?', 'To provide background knowledge for the reader\'s understanding of the argument', 'To introduce unrelated concepts and terms', 'To present the body of the essay before the introduction', 'To conclude the essay introduction', 'A'),
(24, 'How does the author describe the introduction\'s role in an essay?', 'As the early part of the test that begins the discourse', 'As a collection of unrelated ideas with no specific purpose', 'As the foundation of the body, providing background knowledge for the argument raised in the introduction', 'As a stand-alone chapter', 'A'),
(25, 'What is the principal purpose of the body of an essay?', 'To introduce the main argument', 'To provide background information', 'To give evidence for the thesis statement', 'To offer a compelling introduction', 'C'),
(26, 'How is the harmony between paragraphs in the body of an essay achieved?', 'By using different linguistic devices', 'By having the same points of submission', 'By having unrelated points of discussion', 'By using unique linguistic devices', 'A'),
(27, 'What is the unique feature of a concluding paragraph in an essay?', 'It contains a catchy topic sentence', 'It lacks any developmental sentences', 'It serves a similar function as the thesis statement', 'It has a completely different structure', 'C'),
(28, 'What is the purpose of reviewing an essay?', 'To finalize it and prepare for submission', 'To view it critically and make necessary revisions', 'To acknowledge and document citations and references', 'To determine its possible impact on the reader', 'B'),
(29, 'What is one of the essential steps in the review procedure?', 'Comparing the essay with the outline', 'Reading the essay through to understand its content', 'Determining whether the essay is in its final draft', 'Deleting all citations and references', 'A'),
(30, 'How does the text describe the role of language in essay writing?', 'Language is irrelevant and unnecessary in essay writing', 'Language is an inseparable part of essay writing', 'Language can be replaced with other forms of expression', 'Language is only important for minor details', 'B'),
(31, 'In the past, what was one significant way letters were used as a means of communication?', 'For handwriting practice', 'For conveying written messages', 'For sending emails and texts', 'For making phone calls', 'B'),
(32, 'According to Blake and Bly, how is a letter defined in their 1993 statement?', 'A written message sent through email', 'A written conversation on a postcard', 'A written message conveyed from one person to another through a medium', 'A text message', 'C'),
(33, 'Despite modern technology like emails, why is the art of letter writing still considered important today?', 'Because letters are more efficient', 'Because letter writing has been updated with technology', 'Because it\'s fun and helps with handwriting practice', 'Because letter writing is no longer relevant', 'C'),
(34, 'What are the two broad types of letters', 'Business Letters and College Acceptance Letters', 'Formal Letters and Informal Letters', 'Professional Letters and Reminder Letters', 'Email Letters and Text Messages', 'B'),
(35, 'What distinguishes an informal letter from other types of letters, ?', 'It follows a strict formal structure and format', 'It contains personal information and is written to friends', 'It is sent to offices and contains official information', 'It is a letter written for special events', 'B'),
(36, 'Which type of letter is used to convey official information like rules and procedures?', 'Social Letter', 'Informal Letter', 'Circular Letter', 'Official Letter', 'D'),
(37, 'What is the primary purpose of a circular letter?', 'To convey personal information to friends and acquaintances', 'To share official regulations and procedures', 'To inform a large group of people about important information', 'To invite people to social events', 'C'),
(38, 'Why is it essential to identify the type of letter you are writing', 'To determine the length of the letter and make it more effective', 'To decide the color of ink to use when writing the letter', 'To find out the physical address of the recipient', 'To choose whether to use formal or informal language when writing the letter', 'D'),
(39, 'What advice does the text provide about the language used in letters, even in complaint letters?', 'Use harsh and confrontational language to make your point more strongly', 'It\'s not necessary to be polite in complaint letters, so use casual language', 'Always use polite expressions and civil language in all types of letters', 'Use coded language to convey your message indirectly', 'C'),
(40, 'In what part of a formal letter should the date be placed, ?s guidelines?', 'Immediately after the closing statement in the bottom right-hand corner', 'In the middle of the letter, following the main body', 'Immediately after the writer\'s address at the top right-hand side of the letter', 'There is no specific location for the date in a formal letter', 'C'),
(41, 'What is the main distinction between news report writing and academic report writing?', 'News reports focus on opinions and judgments, while academic reports are based solely on facts', 'News reports present facts and expert interviews, while academic reports summarize the research process', 'News reports aim to entertain, while academic reports focus on informing readers', 'News reports start with background information, while academic reports emphasize the most relevant data', 'B'),
(42, 'Which step in writing a report involves drafting the executive summary and table of contents?', 'Step 1: Decide on the Terms of Reference', 'Step 2: Decide on the Procedure', 'Step 7: Make recommendations', 'Step 8: Draft the executive summary and table of contents', 'D'),
(43, 'Why is it important to decide on the terms of reference for your report before starting the writing process?', 'To determine the length of the report', 'To provide a catchy title for the report', 'To decide on the number of references needed in the report', 'To understand the purpose, scope, and audience of the report', 'D'),
(44, 'In which part of a report do you usually find the terms of reference, procedure, findings, conclusions, and recommendations?', 'In the introduction section', 'In the content section', 'In the executive summary section', 'In the references/bibliography section', 'B'),
(45, 'What is the purpose of the procedure section in a report?', 'To list all the books and articles used as references', 'To provide recommendations and suggestions for further research', 'To outline the steps taken to carry out the investigation', 'To summarize the findings of the research', 'C'),
(46, 'What is the typical structure of a report based on the text\'s description?', 'A title page, content, an introduction, and a references/bibliography section', 'An executive summary, a terms of reference section, and appendices', 'An introduction, procedure, and recommendations sections', 'A title page, findings, and conclusions sections', 'C'),
(47, 'Which section of a report usually contains headings and sub-headings numbered by their order in the report\'s structure?', 'The findings section', 'The content section', 'The executive summary section', 'The terms of reference section', 'B'),
(48, 'Why might you include photos, tables, or graphs in a report\'s findings section?', 'To increase the length of the report', 'To overwhelm the reader with visual information', 'To make the report more readable and enhance understanding', 'To add a creative touch to the report', 'C'),
(49, 'What is the purpose of the appendices section in a report?', 'To provide information that couldn\'t fit in the body of the report', 'To include recommendations and conclusions', 'To contain the executive summary and table of contents', 'To list all the sources referred to in the report', 'A'),
(50, 'In the conclusion section, what should you avoid doing?', 'Including a summary of the findings', 'Introducing new information', 'Suggesting possible solutions to the problem', 'Analyzing and interpreting the findings', 'B'),
(51, 'What should be included in the recommendations section of a report?', 'A lengthy discussion of the findings and conclusions', 'A list of practical actions and logical recommendations based on conclusions', 'A description of the background information', 'A detailed account of the research process', 'B'),
(52, 'Why is it recommended to draft the executive summary and table of contents after finishing the report\'s body?', 'To make the reader curious about the report\'s contents', 'To save time by drafting them earlier in the writing process', 'To ensure that they accurately reflect the final structure and recommendations of the report', 'To create a sense of suspense for the reader', 'C'),
(53, 'What is the primary purpose of an executive summary in a report?', 'To introduce new information not covered in the full report', 'To explain the key points of the report and support them with additional evidence', 'To outline the limitations of the report and the assumptions made', 'To discuss the procedure and findings of the report', 'B'),
(54, 'How long should executive summaries typically be in relation to the full report\'s length?', 'About half the length of the full report', 'Around 25% of the full report\'s length', 'Approximately 75% of the full report\'s length', 'Slightly longer than the full report to provide more context', 'A'),
(55, 'What should an executive summary avoid doing when presenting the report\'s main points?', 'Including essential information to help the reader understand the report', 'Introducing the main conclusions or recommendations', 'Overwhelming the reader with excessive information', 'Making the report self-explanatory without requiring further reading', 'C'),
(56, 'Why should recommendations in a report be arranged in order of importance?', 'To show that all recommendations carry equal weight', 'To make the report more interesting for the reader', 'To ensure that the least important recommendations are prioritized first', 'To highlight which actions should be taken first, given their significance', 'D'),
(57, 'In a good report, what role does format play?', 'It should conform to the standard format used in all reports', 'It should follow a random, unique format to stand out', 'It should be irrelevant, as long as the content is accurate and clear', 'It should be convenient and appropriate for the type of report, providing essential components and structure', 'D'),
(58, 'What is the relationship between the length of a report and its effectiveness as a good report?', 'Longer reports are generally more effective because they contain more information', 'Shorter reports are typically more effective because they are easier to read and understand', 'Report length does not affect its effectiveness; it\'s the content and clarity that matter most', 'A good report is both comprehensive and compact, regardless of its length', 'C'),
(59, 'Why is it essential for a good report to be accurate in all aspects?', 'To make the report more challenging and intriguing', 'To provide a concise and self-explanatory document', 'To ensure that repetition of facts and figures is avoided', 'To create a reliable document for decision-making and policy framing', 'D'),
(60, 'What is the importance of using an impersonal style when writing a report?', 'It adds a personal touch to the report, making it more engaging', 'It highlights the author\'s perspective, making the report subjective', 'It makes the report more readable and engaging', 'It focuses on the reader\'s needs rather than the author\'s perspective', 'D'),
(61, 'What essential components should be included in a good report?', 'Only a title and introduction', 'A lengthy discussion and thorough analysis', 'An impersonal style and elaborate explanations', 'The type of report and formatting guidelines', 'B'),
(62, 'Why is it crucial for a good report to be clear and concise in its language?', 'To create a document that is difficult to understand, leaving room for multiple interpretations', 'To make the report lengthy and comprehensive', 'To ensure the report can be understood easily, quickly, and exactly by the reader', 'To add ambiguity and complexity to the report', 'C'),
(63, 'Why is it crucial for a good report to have a proper date and signature?', 'To add a personal touch to the report', 'To make the report more engaging and intriguing', 'For legal significance and as a matter of attention in report writing', 'To emphasize the committee\'s emotions, sentiments, and personal views in the report', 'C'),
(64, 'What is the significance of providing reference to relevant details in a report?', 'To complicate the report and make it harder to understand', 'To ensure that the report is impartial and objective', 'To create an engaging and emotional tone in the report', 'To allow the writer to include their personal views and sentiments in the report', 'B'),
(65, 'Why is it important for a good report to follow an impartial approach?', 'To focus on fault-finding and personal bias', 'To keep emotions, sentiments, and personal views at the forefront in report writing', 'To maintain objectivity and neutrality, avoiding emotions and personal biases', 'To make the report narrow in scope, negative, and destructive in its approach', 'C'),
(66, 'What essential technical details should be considered in a good report?', 'Properly numbered pages and paragraphs, logical and systematic arrangement of parts, clear thinking', 'Random page numbering and illogical organization of parts, biased thinking, and emotional writing', 'Personal sentiments and views, creative formatting, and emotional language', 'Marginal headings and titles, but no need for page numbering and logical arrangement of content', 'A'),
(67, 'What role does presenting a report in a lucid style play in effective report writing?', 'It complicates the report and adds ambiguity', 'It makes the report less interesting and engaging', 'It helps readers understand the report easily and clearly, creating interest with a simple and convincing style', 'It adds emotional language, subjective tone, and illogical arrangement of thoughts to the report', 'C'),
(68, 'What significant shift has occurred in the prominence of the English language in recent years?', 'The decline of the English language as a global means of communication', 'The rise of the English language as the required language for academic instruction, communication, and scholarship globally', 'The increased popularity of other languages over the English language for academic purposes', 'The fading relevance of the English language for global communication', 'B'),
(69, 'In the context of undergraduate students, why is proficiency in academic English language skills essential?', 'To make students more relevant on the global scene', 'To encourage students to use informal language skills for academic tasks', 'To emphasize the importance of using complex sentences and vocabulary in academic writing', 'To promote the use of creative and fictional writing in academic assignments', 'A'),
(70, 'How do some students misinterpret academic writing regarding sentence complexity?', 'They believe academic writing should include simple and clear sentences', 'They think academic writing requires long and complex sentences filled with complicated vocabulary', 'They assume academic writing should only contain short phrases and basic language', 'They are under the impression that academic writing should consist of pictures and diagrams', 'B'),
(71, 'Why is it a misconception that a firm and complete thesis is needed at the outset of academic writing?', 'To encourage students to start writing their papers without any research or planning', 'To emphasize the importance of finalizing the thesis statement before engaging in any research or reflection', 'To promote the idea that the thesis statement should be set in stone and not subject to change throughout the writing process', 'To clarify that academic writing requires no research or analysis, and students should solely rely on their initial ideas', 'B'),
(72, 'What is the misconception concerning the sources students should consult for academic writing?', 'Students believe they should only consult sources that agree with their thesis', 'Students are under the impression that they should rely on sources with diverse viewpoints and arguments', 'Students think they should use sources that support their thesis but can ignore those with opposing views', 'Students assume they need to find sources that align precisely with their thesis without considering opposing perspectives', 'A'),
(73, 'Why is it a misconception to treat quotations from sources as self-explanatory in academic writing?', 'Quotations are inherently self-explanatory and require no further context or explanation', 'Using quotations as the sole content of an academic paper is encouraged and considered sufficient', 'Academic writing does not require analysis or interpretation of quotations; they speak for themselves', 'Quotations need to be used to amplify, reflect, and speak back to a meaningful thesis, not presented in isolation', 'D'),
(74, 'How is the misconception of academic writing as a linear process refuted?', 'Writing is indeed a linear process with distinct starting and ending points', 'Academic writing is entirely disorderly, and there are no defined steps or sections to follow', 'Academic writing involves moving between steps and sections, not following a linear process with set points', 'Academic writing is a rigid process with no room for adjustments or changes as you progress', 'C'),
(75, 'Why do some students hold the misconception that academic writing is a talent only a few are born with?', 'Because they believe that being a genius writer is entirely within their control and determined by their efforts', 'Because they think they are naturally talented writers and do not need to work on improving their skills', 'Because they believe that academic writing skills are innate and cannot be developed through study and practice', 'Because they believe that natural writing talent is a prerequisite for academic writing, and they must be born with this talent', 'C'),
(76, 'According to research in writing studies, what can be concluded about writing skills?', 'Writing skills are natural talents and cannot be taught to students at any level', 'Writing skills can be taught to students at all levels, and being born with a flair for writing is essential to excel in academic writing', 'Writing skills cannot be improved, and being born with a talent for writing is the only path to success in academic writing', 'Writing skills are irrelevant in academic writing, and the emphasis should be on innate writing talent as the sole requirement for success', 'B'),
(77, 'What is the primary objective of academic writing?', 'To promote personal opinions without supporting arguments', 'To convey complex ideas, processes, procedures, critiques, and arguments on a given topic in a structured manner', 'To create a diverse set of writing styles and formats to challenge traditional academic conventions', 'To focus solely on presenting known information without any new perspectives or ideas', 'B'),
(78, 'In academic writing, how should an argument be presented?', 'As an unsupported personal opinion', 'As a carefully arranged and supported viewpoint intended to engage readers in a conversation', 'As a complex, convoluted statement filled with technical jargon and unclear language', 'As a rigid, fixed statement without any room for flexibility or alternative viewpoints', 'B'),
(79, 'What is the primary focus of academic writing in terms of analysis?', 'Mainly addressing \"what\" questions', 'Primarily seeking answers to \"how\" and \"why\" questions related to a topic', 'Exclusively exploring the relationship between various ideas and perspectives', 'Solely presenting arguments without any form of analysis or inquiry into the topic', 'B'),
(80, 'Why is clear and lucid communication essential in academic writing?', 'To overwhelm the reader with complex language and ideas', 'To bore the reader with a monotonous tone and straightforward writing style', 'To engage the reader and effectively communicate ideas by presenting them clearly and with the reader in mind', 'To confuse the reader by using imprecise word choices and sentence structures', 'C'),
(81, 'What does precision in academic writing involve?', 'Using language that conveys the intended meaning clearly and unambiguously', 'Utilizing complex words and phrases to make the writing appear more scholarly and authoritative', 'Including vague and uncertain language to create a sense of mystery and intrigue', 'Employing contradictions and irrelevancies to add depth and complexity to the writing', 'A'),
(82, 'What is the primary purpose of the introduction in academic writing?', 'To present a broad overview of the topic with irrelevant information', 'To introduce the thesis, contextualize the subject, and set the scope of the essay', 'To provide a detailed analysis of the topic that exceeds the main body\'s scope', 'To restate the entire essay\'s content in a more concise manner and finalize the essay\'s conclusion', 'B'),
(83, 'What is the role of each paragraph in the main body of an academic essay?', 'To present a singular core argument or idea supported by relevant examples and evidence', 'To summarize the entire essay\'s content in a concise form, giving a final view on the topic', 'To briefly mention the most important points and arguments without providing detailed explanations', 'To list the references and citations used in the essay, ensuring proper credit to the original authors of the sources cited', 'A'),
(84, 'Why is the conclusion in academic writing often considered the most important part of the essay?', 'Because it contains the thesis statement and sets the direction for the entire essay', 'Because it provides an in-depth analysis and a detailed summary of the essay\'s content', 'Because it tends to be shorter and more varied in format than the introduction, effectively summarizing the arguments and showing that the original question has been answered', 'Because it introduces the reader to the topic and states the problem that will be addressed in the essay', 'C'),
(85, 'What is the primary purpose of the conclusion in academic writing?', 'To introduce the reader to the topic and state the problem to be addressed in the essay', 'To outline the thesis statement and set the direction for the entire essay', 'To effectively summarize the arguments, make final comments, and leave the reader with a sense of closure', 'To contain a detailed analysis of the essay\'s content, including all evidence and examples from the main body', 'C'),
(86, 'Which components are typically included in research projects, in addition to essays?', 'Title page, acknowledgements, and table of contents', 'Literature review, theoretical framework, and findings/discussion', 'Conclusion, references, and appendices', 'All of the above', 'D'),
(87, 'What is the potential consequence of plagiarism in academic writing?', 'A high grade for the course', 'A fail grade in the course', 'An opportunity to resubmit the assignment with proper citations', 'A commendation from the university for original research', 'B'),
(88, 'What is self-plagiarism, and why is it considered a breach of academic integrity?', 'Submitting the same work for different courses', 'Borrowing ideas from another author without proper citations', 'Submitting your work without properly citing your sources', 'Using sources from different authors to support your ideas', 'A'),
(89, 'What is collusion in the context of academic integrity, and what are the consequences?', 'Collusion is working collaboratively with other students on a group project', 'Collusion is a form of misrepresentation or alteration of data', 'Collusion occurs when more than one student contributes to a piece of academic writing originally assigned as an individual task', 'Collusion is the practice of sharing academic resources with peers', 'C'),
(90, 'What is the essence of fabrication in academic writing, and how does it compromise academic integrity?', 'Fabrication involves referencing sources in a clear and transparent manner', 'Fabrication is the process of collating data from multiple sources', 'Fabrication is a form of misrepresentation or alteration of data in academic writing, often involving falsifying data and figures', 'Fabrication relates to writing about fictional content', 'C'),
(91, 'What is the main purpose of referencing and documentation in academic writing?', 'To make your paper seem more credible and sophisticated', 'To acknowledge sources, respect other people\'s work, and help readers find additional materials about your topic', 'To avoid the need for citations and maintain originality in your work', 'To showcase your command of the English language', 'B'),
(92, 'Which referencing styles are mentioned as common in academic writing?', 'APA and MLA', 'Harvard and Chicago', 'AMA and Chicago', 'MLA and Harvard', 'A'),
(93, 'What does APA stand for in academic writing, and which fields is it primarily used in?', 'APA stands for American Psychological Association and is mainly used in education, psychology, and social sciences.', 'APA stands for American Publication Association and is primarily used in literature and the arts.', 'APA stands for Academic Publication and is mainly used in medicine, health, and biological sciences.', 'APA stands for Author and Publisher Alliance and is primarily used in business studies.', 'A'),
(94, 'In APA in-text citations, what is the purpose of parenthetical citations?', 'To provide additional information that expands upon the original citation.', 'To explain the author\'s personal experiences with the topi', 'To serve as footnotes to elaborate on specific terms or phrases used in the text.', 'To alert the reader about sources that have informed the author\'s writing and locate corresponding entries in the reference list.', 'D'),
(95, 'What is the recommended font and font size for academic papers in APA format?', 'Times New Roman 14pt', 'Arial 11pt', 'Georgia 10pt', 'Comic Sans 12pt', 'B'),
(96, 'Which section should you include in an undergraduate paper in APA format?', 'Title Page, Main Body, and Abstract', 'Introduction, Main Body, and Conclusion', 'Acknowledgements, Methodology, and Discussion', 'Title Page, Methodology, and References', 'B'),
(97, 'In APA format, what is the purpose of a hanging indent in the reference list?', 'To make the text more visually appealing', 'To separate each reference entry from the text for better organization', 'To create a list of hanging references that can be removed if necessary', 'To highlight the key elements of a reference entry and make them stand out', 'B'),
(98, 'How should you alphabetize the reference list entries in APA format when the author is unknown?', 'Alphabetize based on the publication date of the sources', 'Ignore alphabetization and list all unknown author sources at the end of the reference list', 'Alphabetize by the first word in the title of the source, excluding \"a,\" \"an,\" and \"the\"', 'Arrange them according to the order in which they were cited in the text', 'C'),
(99, 'What is the recommended format for citing sources in APA format regarding the author\'s name and initials?', 'First name, middle name, and last name followed by a period (e.g., John M. Smith)', 'Last name followed by a period, and then the initials with no periods (e.g., Smith J M)', 'Last name followed by a comma, and then the first and middle initials with periods (e.g., Smith, J. M.)', 'The entire name, including the title and honorifics, with initials omitted (e.g., Dr. John Michael Smith, Jr.)', 'C'),
(100, 'Why is it important to include all the sources you cite in your reference list in APA format?', 'To make your reference list look comprehensive and impressive', 'To ensure you have a backup list in case you need to add more sources later', 'To document all the facts and figures that are common knowledge without the need for citation', 'To provide full citation details of the works you have consulted, used in your paper, and allow readers to access them', 'D'),
(101, 'What should you do when you need to cite a source that is no longer recoverable in APA format?', 'Include a brief description of the source\'s content in the citation and proceed to use it in your paper', 'Ignore the source entirely in your text and reference list', 'Avoid using any online sources in your academic writing to prevent such issues', 'Credit the source in the text, but avoid listing it in the reference list if it cannot be retrieved', 'D'),
(102, 'What should you do if the copyright date on a book\'s copyright page differs from the release date in APA format?', 'Use the copyright date for the year of publication in the reference entry', 'Use the release date for the year of publication in the reference entry', 'Include both the copyright date and the release date in the reference entry to cover both possibilities', 'Skip the date entirely in the reference entry, focusing only on the title and author\'s name', 'A'),
(103, 'How should you format the names of authors when a book has multiple authors in APA format?', 'Separate author names with semicolons (e.g., Smith; Johnson; Davis)', 'List all author names consecutively without any punctuation (e.g., Smith Johnson Davis)', 'Place a comma and an ampersand (&) before the name of the last author cited (e.g., Smith, Johnson, & Davis)', 'Add \"and others\" after the first author\'s name to represent the rest of the authors (e.g., Smith and others)', 'C'),
(104, 'In APA format, how should you format the reference list entry for a book with a corporate or group author?', 'List the members of the corporate author separately in the reference entry', 'Include only the name of the organization as the author\'s name in the reference entry', 'Use the full name of the organization as the author\'s name in the reference entry and follow the usual format for other details', 'Omit the corporate author\'s name entirely from the reference entry', 'C'),
(105, 'What format should you use when there is no author or creator provided for a source in APA format?', 'Start the citation with the title/name of the item, followed by the date of publication and other citation details', 'Include the words \"No Author\" in place of the author\'s name in the reference entry', 'Begin the citation with the date of publication, followed by the title/name of the item and other citation details', 'Simply skip the entry and do not include it in the reference list', 'A'),
(106, 'How should you list the editors of a book in APA format when there is more than one editor?', 'List the names of editors in alphabetical order, separated by semicolons (e.g., Smith; Johnson; Davis)', 'Separate the editors\' names with commas, without any punctuation between the last editor\'s name and the preceding commas (e.g., Smith, Johnson, Davis)', 'List the names of editors consecutively with no specific punctuation between them (e.g., Smith Johnson Davis)', 'Place an ampersand (&) before the last editor\'s name to indicate it is the final one (e.g., Smith, Johnson, & Davis)', 'D'),
(107, 'How should you format the DOI (Digital Object Identifier) in a journal article reference in APA format?', 'Include the DOI in the format \"https://doi.org/10.xxxx\" at the end of the reference entry (e.g., https://doi.org/10.xxxx)', 'Place the DOI in parentheses with \"DOI\" preceding it at the end of the reference entry (e.g., DOI: 10.xxxx)', 'Use the DOI as an additional reference entry following the main one for the article (e.g., separate DOI reference)', 'Omit the DOI completely from the reference entry', 'A'),
(108, 'In APA format, how should you include the volume and issue numbers when citing a journal article with multiple authors?', 'Include the volume number only, as issue numbers are not required in the reference entry (e.g., Vol. 22)', 'Include both the volume number and issue number in parentheses, separated by a comma (e.g., Vol. 22, Issue 1)', 'List the issue number only, as volume numbers are not needed for journal articles (e.g., Issue 1)', 'Place the volume and issue numbers in brackets before the journal name (e.g., [Vol. 22, Issue 1] Trends in TESOL)', 'B'),
(109, 'In MLA format, how should you format the title of a source that is part of a larger whole (container)?', 'Italicize the title of the source', 'Enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(110, 'When there are other contributors to a source besides the author (e.g., editors, illustrators), how should you include their names in MLA documentation?', 'Include their names after the title of the source and precede each name with \"Contributed by\" (e.g., Contributed by John Smith, Mary Brown)', 'Add their names in parentheses immediately after the title of the source, with \"Contributors\" preceding the names (e.g., Contributors: John Smith, Mary Brown)', 'List their names in a separate section at the end of your paper, after the Works Cited list under \"Other Contributors\"', 'Include their names after the author\'s name, separated by a comma, and use \"and\" before the last contributor\'s name (e.g., Author: John Smith and Mary Brown)', 'D'),
(111, 'In MLA documentation, how should you format the publisher\'s information?', 'Include the full name and location of the publisher, followed by a comma and the publication date of the source (e.g., Publisher: ABC Publishing, New York, 2020)', 'List only the publication date of the source, omitting the publisher\'s information entirely (e.g., Publication Date: 2020)', 'Include the name of the publisher in quotation marks and follow it with the publication date (e.g., Publisher: \"ABC Publishing\" 2020)', 'Provide only the location of the publisher without including the publisher\'s name or the publication date (e.g., Publisher Location: New York)', 'A'),
(112, 'When should you provide the location of a source in MLA documentation?', 'Only include the location if the source is a physical print edition (e.g., a book)', 'Include the location for all types of sources to help readers identify where they can access the source', 'Include the location when it is not possible to determine the publication date of the source', 'Omit the location in all cases, as it is not required in MLA citation and documentation', 'A'),
(113, 'In MLA format, how should you format the title of a source that is part of a larger whole (container)?', 'Italicize the title of the source', 'Enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(114, 'How should you format the title of a container in MLA citation and documentation?', 'Italicize the title of the container', 'Enclose the title of the container in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the container in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(115, 'In MLA documentation, how should you format the title of a source that is part of a larger whole (container)?', 'Italicize the title of the source', 'Enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(116, 'In MLA format, how should you format the title of a source that is part of a larger whole (container)?', 'Italicize the title of the source', 'Enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the source in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(117, 'How should you format the title of a container in MLA citation and documentation?', 'Italicize the title of the container', 'Enclose the title of the container in double quotation marks', 'Both italicize and enclose the title of the container in double quotation marks', 'Capitalize the first letter of each word in the title without using any special formatting', 'C'),
(118, 'In MIA document format, how should you format the title of your paper or any headings within the paper?', 'Use bold formatting for the title and all headings, including italics for emphasis', 'Apply italics to the title and headings, with bold for added emphasis', 'Write the title and headings in Title Case (standard capitalization) without using bold, underline, italics, or quotation marks', 'Capitalize the title and headings with bold formatting for emphasis', 'C'),
(119, 'How should quotes longer than 4 lines be presented in MIA document format?', 'Include them within the text, maintaining double spacing throughout, and enclosing them in double quotation marks', 'Format them as a block of text indented half an inch from the left margin and maintain double spacing throughout', 'Incorporate them into the text without special formatting, and single space them throughout', 'Place them within the text with italics for emphasis and enclose them in double quotation marks', 'B'),
(120, 'What should be the margin size for an MIA document format?', 'Set the margins to 1 inch on all sides', 'Use 1.5-inch margins for the top and bottom and 1-inch margins for the left and right sides', 'Keep the left margin at 0.5 inches and all other margins at 1 inch', 'Use 0.5-inch margins on all sides', 'A'),
(121, 'How should the first line of each paragraph be indented in MIA document format?', 'One-inch indentation from the left margin', 'Half-inch indentation from the left margin', 'No indentation of the first line, aligning it with the left margin', 'Two-inch indentation from the left margin', 'B'),
(122, 'What is the recommended font style and size in MIA document format?', 'Any easily readable typeface with no specific font size recommendation', 'Times New Roman, 12 points, with italics for emphasis', 'Comic Sans MS, 14 points, with bold formatting for emphasis', 'Arial, 10 points, with underline for emphasis', 'C'),
(123, 'In an MLA Works Cited list, what is the proper format for an eBook with two authors?', 'Last name of first author, first name, and first name last name of second author. Title of the book: Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'Last name of both authors, followed by their first names. Title of the eBook in italics. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher\'s full name, year of publication.', 'Last name, first name of the first author, and first name last name of the second author. Title of the eBook Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'Last name of the first author, et al. Title of the eBook Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'C'),
(124, 'How should you format the entry for an eBook with one author in an MLA Works Cited list?', 'Include the author\'s last name, first name, the title of the eBook in quotation marks, edition if given and is not the first edition, the name of the publisher in full, and the year of publication.', 'List the author\'s first name followed by their last name, the title of the eBook in italics, edition if given and is not the first edition, a shortened publisher name, and the year of publication.', 'Include only the author\'s last name, the title of the eBook in italics, the edition if given and is not the first edition, the full name of the publisher, and the year of publication.', 'Use the author\'s last name, first name. Title of the eBook in italics. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'C'),
(125, 'In an MLA Works Cited list, how should you format the entry for a book with three or more authors?', 'Last name first name of the first author, et al. Title of the book Subtitle if any, Edition if given and is not the first edition, Publisher name often shortened, year of publication', 'List the names of all authors in the order they appear on the book, with the first author\'s name in \"Last name, first name\" format and subsequent authors in \"First name last name\" format. Include the title in italics, edition if given and is not the first edition, and the publisher\'s full name.', 'Include only the first author\'s name followed by the title in quotation marks. Omit the edition information, provide the publisher\'s full name, and the year of publication.', 'Use \"Author et al.\" with no first author\'s name, enclose the title in double quotation marks, include the edition if given and is not the first edition, the publisher name in full, and the year of publication.', 'A'),
(126, 'How should the entries in an MLA Works Cited list be organized?', 'Organize them by the order of citation within the paper, without any specific order or grouping.', 'Arrange the entries alphabetically by the first author\'s last name, with each entry beginning flush with the left margin.', 'Group the entries by type (e.g., books, articles, websites) and then alphabetize each group by the first author\'s last name.', 'Prioritize entries by the year of publication, with the most recent sources listed first, followed by older publications.', 'B'),
(127, 'What is the recommended indentation style for a Works Cited list in MLA format when an entry runs more than one line?', 'Use a block indentation style, with the first line flush with the left margin and subsequent lines indented by half an inch.', 'No indentation should be applied to subsequent lines of an entry that runs more than one line.', 'All lines of a multi-line entry should be indented by one inch from the left margin.', 'Apply a hanging indent style, with the first line flush with the left margin and subsequent lines indented by half an inch.', 'D'),
(128, 'In the MIA document format, what is the recommended font style and size for the paper text?', 'Comic Sans MS, 10 points', 'Times New Roman, 12 points with standard spacing', 'Arial, 14 points with italics for emphasis', 'Helvetica, 10 points with bold formatting for emphasis', 'B'),
(129, 'How should you format the first line of each paragraph in the MIA document format?', 'Without any indentation, align it with the left margin', 'Apply a one-inch indentation from the left margin', 'Set a one-centimeter indentation from the right margin', 'Utilize a two-inch indentation from the left margin', 'A'),
(130, 'In an MLA Works Cited list, what is the correct way to format a book in print with two authors?', 'Last name of first author, first name, and first name last name of the second author. Title of the book: Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'Last name of both authors, followed by their first names. Title of the book in italics. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher\'s full name, year of publication.', 'Last name, first name of the first author, and first name last name of the second author. Title of the book Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'Last name of the first author, et al. Title of the book Subtitle if any. Edition if given and is not the first edition, publisher name often shortened, year of publication.', 'C'),
(131, 'In an MLA Works Cited list, how should the entries be organized?', 'Organize them by the order of citation within the paper, without any specific order or grouping.', 'Arrange the entries alphabetically by the first author\'s last name, with each entry beginning flush with the left margin.', 'Group the entries by type (e.g., books, articles, websites) and then alphabetize each group by the first author\'s last name.', 'Prioritize entries by the year of publication, with the most recent sources listed first, followed by older publications.', 'B'),
(132, 'What should be the indentation style for a Works Cited list in MLA format when an entry runs more than one line?', 'Use a block indentation style, with the first line flush with the left margin and subsequent lines indented by half an inch.', 'No indentation should be applied to subsequent lines of an entry that runs more than one line.', 'All lines of a multi-line entry should be indented by one inch from the left margin.', 'Apply a hanging indent style, with the first line flush with the left margin and subsequent lines indented by half an inch.', 'D');
INSERT INTO `gst_102` (`id`, `question`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `answer`) VALUES
(133, 'In an MLA Works Cited list, how should a book with three or more authors be formatted?', 'Include only the first author\'s name, followed by the title in quotation marks. Omit the edition information, provide the publisher\'s full name, and the year of publication.', 'Last name first name of the first author, et al. Title of the book Subtitle if any, Edition if given and is not the first edition, Publisher name often shortened, year of publication', 'List the names of all authors in the order they appear on the book, with the first author\'s name in \"Last name, first name\" format and subsequent authors in \"First name last name\" format. Include the title in italics, edition if given and is not the first edition, and the publisher\'s full name.', 'Use \"Author et al.\" with no first author\'s name, enclose the title in double quotation marks, include the edition if given and is not the first edition, the publisher name in full, and the year of publication.', 'B'),
(134, 'In the MLA Works Cited format, how should the first line of each entry be aligned?', 'One inch from the left margin', 'Flush with the left margin', 'Half an inch from the left margin', 'Centered on the page, aligned with the top margin', 'B'),
(135, 'In an MLA citation for a journal article in print with one author, which information comes after the title of the article?', 'The issue number of the journal', 'The publication date and the page numbers', 'The volume number of the journal and the DOI', 'The author\'s last name, first name, and the journal name', 'B'),
(136, 'When citing a journal article in MLA format, what does \"vol. volume number, no. issue number\" signify in the citation?', 'The volume number of the journal and the publication date', 'The publication date of the article and the volume number of the journal', 'The volume number of the journal and the issue number of the journal', 'The title of the article and the publication date', 'C'),
(137, 'In the MLA format for an online journal article with one author, where should the article\'s DOI be placed in the citation?', 'Before the volume number of the journal', 'After the author\'s last name and first name, separated by a comma', 'After the last page number of the article', 'Before the name of the journal but after the title of the article', 'B'),
(138, 'How should you format an online journal article with two authors in MLA style?', 'Include only the first author\'s name followed by \"et al.\" and the title in italics. Provide the volume number and page numbers.', 'List both authors in \"Last name, first name\" format and the title in quotation marks. Include the volume and issue number, along with the page range.', 'Use the first author\'s last name and first name, followed by \"and\" and the second author\'s name in \"Last name, First name\" format. Include the title in italics and the DOI.', 'Include the first author\'s name in \"Last name, First name\" format, followed by \"and\" and the second author\'s name. Add the title in italics and the publication date.', 'C'),
(139, 'When citing a newspaper article from a website in MLA format, what should be included after the article\'s title?', 'The URL of the website and the date it was accessed by the reader', 'The publication date of the article and the newspaper name', 'The author\'s last name and the page number of the article', 'The title of the website and the publication date of the article', 'A'),
(140, 'In an MLA citation for a newspaper article from a website, how should the date of publication be formatted?', 'Year, Month, Day (e.g., 2019, September 25)', 'Day, Month, Year (e.g., 25 September 2019)', 'Month, Day, Year (e.g., September 25, 2019)', 'Year (e.g., 2019)', 'A'),
(141, 'In an MLA citation for a newspaper article in print, where should the page number be placed in the citation?', 'After the title of the article', 'Before the author\'s name and last name', 'After the publication date and the newspaper name', 'After the date of publication and before the name of the newspaper', 'D'),
(142, 'How should you format the date of publication in an MLA citation for a newspaper article in print?', 'Year, Month Day (e.g., 2019, September 25)', 'Month, Day, Year (e.g., September 25, 2019)', 'Day, Month, Year (e.g., 25 September 2019)', 'Year (e.g., 2019)', 'D'),
(143, 'In an APA citation for a book, where should the year of publication be placed?', 'After the title of the book', 'Before the author\'s last name and first name', 'After the publisher\'s name and location', 'After the author\'s name but before the title of the book', 'A'),
(144, 'What should be included in an APA citation for a book as the first element?', 'The edition of the book', 'The publisher\'s name and location', 'The title of the book in italics', 'The author\'s last name, first name, and initials', 'D'),
(145, 'What is the primary purpose of the summary section in passages in English?', 'Test the candidate\'s ability to understand the entire passage', 'Test total comprehension of the passage', 'Test the candidate\'s personal interpretations and insights', 'Test the length of the original passage', 'A'),
(146, 'What should a summary include, ?', 'Personal comments and interpretations', 'Vital details', 'Unnecessary details', 'The exact words of the original passage', 'B'),
(147, 'What is a \"Topical Sentence\" in summary writing?', 'The thesis statement of the author', 'The main ideas in the original passage', 'The first or second sentence in a paragraph', 'A concise statement of the passage\'s main point', 'C'),
(148, 'When condensing a passage, what is a way to reduce wordiness?', 'Omit repetition and important details', 'Use more lengthy sentences and phrases', 'Keep all the original examples, incidents, and descriptions', 'Combine two or more sentences into one concise sentence', 'D'),
(149, 'What is the importance of using one\'s own words in summary writing?', 'It adds creativity to the summary', 'It makes the summary longer and more detailed', 'It\'s a way to lift paragraphs from the original passage', 'It makes the summary concise and avoids plagiarism', 'D'),
(150, 'In summary writing, what should you do when a main idea is implied rather than stated directly?', 'Omit it from the summary', 'Add your personal interpretation', 'Phrase a statement that expresses it', 'Include it in the original words', 'C'),
(151, 'What does the term \"comprehension\" mean?', 'Understanding', 'Reading speed', 'Memorization', 'Vocabulary', 'A'),
(152, 'What is an important aspect of a candidate\'s skill in reading and comprehending a passage, ?', 'Memorization', 'Speed', 'Attention to every detail', 'Summarizing', 'B'),
(153, 'What are the basic skills required in comprehension, ?', 'Ability to summarize passages and memorize details', 'Identifying figures of speech and emotions', 'Making implied references and recognizing grammatical functions', 'None of the above', 'C'),
(154, 'what should you do when reading the questions for a comprehension passage?', 'Read them after you\'ve read the passage', 'Skip the questions and read the passage first', 'Make sure your full concentration is on the passage', 'Read them carefully', 'D'),
(155, 'In answering comprehension questions, what should you avoid doing', 'Giving extra information not required', 'Sharing personal opinions and emotions', 'Showing emotions towards characters in the passage', 'Providing additional information', 'A'),
(156, 'What is sovereign right', 'Ownership of properties', 'Administrative control by political authority', 'Private person\'s right over belongings', 'Supreme rulership over a state', 'B'),
(157, 'what is the ultimate right of control vested in?', 'The parliament of the federation and the state legislatures', 'The council chief and council of elders', 'The traditional authority of the area concerned', 'The village head and whole community through family heads', 'A'),
(158, 'which of the following items remains yours as long as the state doesn\'t have an interest in it for the common good?', 'Furniture', 'Clothes', 'Vehicle', 'Immovable property', 'D'),
(159, 'What does APA stand for in the context of citation styles?', 'American Publication Association', 'American Professional Authors', 'American Psychological Association', 'Author\'s Publication Authority', 'C'),
(160, 'Which disciplines mainly use the Harvard citation style?', 'Social sciences, law, natural sciences, medicine', 'English, humanities, social and behavioral sciences', 'Linguistics, modern languages, social sciences', 'Physics, chemistry, mathematics', 'A'),
(161, 'What does MLA stand for, and where is it primarily used?', 'Modern Language Association; English, humanities', 'Modern Linguistics Association; Social and behavioral sciences', 'Multinational Language Authority; English and other languages', 'Mathematics and Logic; science disciplines', 'A'),
(162, 'Which disciplines use Chicago/Turabian citation style?', 'Social sciences, history, political studies, theology', 'Mathematics, physics, computer science, engineering', 'Business, economics, law, medicine', 'English, literature, philosophy', 'A'),
(163, 'What is the primary method of citation used in APA style?', 'Footnotes in-text citations', 'Parenthetical in-text citations', 'Endnotes in-text citations', 'Bibliographic endnotes', 'B'),
(164, 'In APA style, when do you include page numbers in the text?', 'For all in-text citations', 'Only for direct quotations', 'For paraphrased material', 'Page numbers are not used in APA style', 'B'),
(165, 'What is the rule for capitalizing words in the title of a source in APA references?', 'Capitalize all words with five or more letters in the title', 'Capitalize all words in the title', 'Capitalize only the first word of the title', 'Capitalize every noun in the title', 'A'),
(166, 'How should you format an APA in-text citation when directly quoting from a work?', 'Use the author\'s last name followed by the date of publication in parentheses', 'Use the title of the work followed by the publication year in brackets', 'Use the page number followed by the publication date in brackets', 'Use the publication year followed by the author\'s last name in brackets', 'A'),
(167, 'What is the format for long quotations in APA style?', 'Enclose them in double quotation marks', 'Place them in a free-standing block, indenting 1/2 inch from the left margin', 'Include them as regular text with no special formatting', 'Use italics for long quotations', 'B'),
(168, 'What is the correct format for a parenthetical citation of a long quotation in APA style?', 'Place it before the quotation with quotation marks', 'Place it after the quotation with quotation marks', 'Include it at the beginning of the quotation', 'Omit it from the text completely', 'B'),
(169, 'What does MLA stand for in the context of citation styles?', 'Modern Literature Association', 'Modern Linguistic Authority', 'Modern Language Association', 'Modern Learning Academy', 'C'),
(170, 'In which academic disciplines is the MLA style of writing mainly used?', 'Mathematics and Sciences', 'English and the Humanities', 'Social and Behavioral Sciences', 'Law and Political Studies', 'B'),
(171, 'In grammar, the sentence that makes a statement is called ________', 'interrogative', 'exclamation', 'declarative', 'imperative', 'C'),
